<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-the-mean-value-theorem" class="notes">
<title>3.7 The Mean Value Thoerem</title>
<introduction>
    <p>
      The Mean Value Theorem (MVT) connects the average rate of change of a function over 
      an interval to its instantaneous rate of change at a specific point within that interval. 
      It serves as a foundational building block for many core concepts in calculus, 
      including Rolle's Theorem and the proof of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-mvt">
    <title>Mean Value Theorem</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
     If <m>f</m> is continuous on a closed interval <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on the open interval <m>(a,b)</m>, then there exists a number <m>c</m> in <m>(a,b)</m> such that
          <me> f'(c)=\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}, </me>
          or, equivalently,
          <me> f(b)-f(a)=f'(c)(b-a). </me>
    </p>
    </statement>
    </theorem>

<example xml:id="ex-mvt-quadratic">
  <statement>
    <p> If <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{4}x^{2}+1</m>, show that <m>f</m> satisfies the hypothesis of the Mean Value Theorem on the interval <m>[-1,4]</m>, 
        and find a number <m>c</m> in <m>(-1,4)</m> that satisfies the conclusion of the theorem. </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p> First, we must check the two hypotheses of the Mean Value Theorem: </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li> <p> The function <m>f(x)</m> is a polynomial, so it is continuous everywhere, 
            including on the closed interval <m>[-1,4]</m>. </p> </li>
        <li> <p> The derivative is <m>f'(x) = \frac{1}{2}x</m>, which exists everywhere, 
            meaning <m>f</m> is differentiable on the open interval <m>(-1,4)</m>. </p> </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since both hypotheses are satisfied, there exists a number <m>c</m> in <m>(-1,4)</m> such that:
      <me>f'(c) = \frac{f(4) - f(-1)}{4 - (-1)}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, we evaluate the function at the endpoints:
      <me>f(4) = \frac{1}{4}(4)^2 + 1 = 5</me>
      <me>f(-1) = \frac{1}{4}(-1)^2 + 1 = \frac{5}{4}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Now, calculate the average rate of change:
      <me>\frac{5 - \frac{5}{4}}{4 - (-1)} = \frac{\frac{15}{4}}{5} = \frac{3}{4}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, set <m>f'(c) = \frac{3}{4}</m> and solve for <m>c</m>:
      <me>\frac{1}{2}c = \frac{3}{4} \implies c = \frac{3}{2}</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since <m>c = \frac{3}{2}</m> (or <m>1.5</m>) lies within the open interval <m>(-1,4)</m>, this value satisfies the conclusion of the theorem.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="exer-sine-mvt-proof">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Prove that if <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are any real numbers, then
      <me>|\sin u -\sin v|\leq |u-v|.</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <title>Hint</title>
      Apply the Mean Value Theorem with <m>f(x)=\sin x</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      If <m>u = v</m>, then <m>|\sin u - \sin v| = 0</m> and <m>|u - v| = 0</m>. The inequality holds as <m>0 \leq 0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without loss of generality, assume <m>u \lt v</m>. Let <m>f(x) = \sin x</m> on the closed interval <m>[u, v]</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      We check the hypotheses of the Mean Value Theorem:
      <ol>
        <li>
        <p>
        The function <m>f(x) = \sin x</m> is continuous everywhere, so it is continuous on <m>[u, v]</m>.
        </p>
        </li>
        <li>
        <p>
        The derivative <m>f'(x) = \cos x</m> exists everywhere, so <m>f</m> is differentiable on <m>(u, v)</m>.
        </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
      By the Mean Value Theorem, there exists a number <m>c</m> in <m>(u, v)</m> such that:
      <me>\frac{\sin v - \sin u}{v - u} = \cos c</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Taking the absolute value of both sides gives:
      <me>\left| \frac{\sin v - \sin u}{v - u} \right| = |\cos c|</me>
      <me>\frac{|\sin u - \sin v|}{|u - v|} = |\cos c|</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      We know that for any real number <m>c</m>, <m>|\cos c| \leq 1</m>. Substituting this inequality yields:
      <me>\frac{|\sin u - \sin v|}{|u - v|} \leq 1</me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Multiplying both sides by the positive quantity <m>|u - v|</m> completes the proof:
      <me>|\sin u - \sin v| \leq |u - v| \qquad \blacksquare</me>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

</section>
